From 3fd7db1e200339a89662b2344e0d9ed8de13b43b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 20:40:06 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] GBM: Dolby Vision Low-Latency also where the TV takes
 Standard, and its target set by hand

Low-Latency (player-led) went out only to a TV input that offers no
other ("A display that takes Dolby Vision Standard keeps it, whatever
this says"). CoreELEC offers player-led as a choice for any display
(its "dolbyvisionled", p3i's "dolbyvision.type", avdvplus's automatic
choice, read as data), and owners of projectors and of HDFury chains
ask for it: the mapping of the player in place of that of the display,
to the peak and the colours that the display has, not those it
declares.

Under "Dolby Vision Low-Latency (player-led)", at the expert level:
 - "Low-Latency also where the TV takes Standard"
   (videoscreen.hadesdvllalways): Low-Latency to a display that takes
   Standard too, where its Dolby VSVDB offers Low-Latency; the mapping
   is then that of Hades (the BT.2390 curve guided by each scene's level
   1 metadata), as for a display of Low-Latency only;
 - "Low-Latency: peak and colours set here" (videoscreen.hadesdvllmanual)
   with the peak (40 to 4000 cd/m2) and the gamut (BT.2020, DCI-P3,
   BT.709) that Low-Latency is mapped to, in place of those of the
   VSVDB.
Both off by default: they change the picture. The black of the source
is kept, as before (a black target would change the shading of every
dark scene, and is not measured). kodi.log says the target and where
it comes from, and when Low-Latency goes to a display that takes
Standard. English and Spanish; the help of Low-Latency says it.
